{"name":"Carolina","hex":"#cbefcb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Carolina","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Latin, Latinate form of Caroline; rare in English.","A placename:\nEither North Carolina or South Carolina."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Carolina"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carolinas","blocks":[{"text":"The Carolinas are the U.S. states of North Carolina and South Carolina considered together as a historical or cultural entity. They are bordered by Virginia to the north, Tennessee to the west, and Georgia to the southwest. The Atlantic Ocean is to the east and south."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Carolinas.svg/330px-Carolinas.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carolinas","wikidata":"Q2747220"},{"title":"North Carolina","blocks":[{"text":"North Carolina is a state in the Southeastern and South Atlantic regions of the United States. It is bordered by Virginia to the north, the Atlantic Ocean to the east, South Carolina to the south, Georgia to the southwest, and Tennessee to the west. The state is the 28th-largest and ninth-most populous of the United States. Along with South Carolina, it makes up the Carolinas region of the East Coast. At the 2020 census, the state had a population of 10,439,388. Raleigh is the state's capital and Charlotte is its most populous and one of the fastest growing cities in the United States. The Charlotte metropolitan area, with an estimated population of 2,883,370 in 2024, is the most populous metropolitan area in North Carolina, the 21st-most populous in the United States, and the largest banking center in the nation after New York City. The Research Triangle, with an estimated population of 2,368,947 in 2023, is the second-most populous combined metropolitan area in the state, 31st-most populous in the United States, and is home to the largest research park in the United States, Research Triangle Park."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg/330px-Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Carolina","wikidata":"Q1454"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg/500px-King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg/1280px-King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg/500px-King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Carolinas are named after King Charles I of England. Carolina is taken from the Latin word for \"Charles\", Carolus.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKing_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Carolinacolony.png/500px-Carolinacolony.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Carolinacolony.png/500px-Carolinacolony.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 1663 Province of Carolina Charter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarolinacolony.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Uptown_Charlotte_2018_taking_by_DJI_Phantom_4_pro.jpg/500px-Uptown_Charlotte_2018_taking_by_DJI_Phantom_4_pro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm